From: What-A-Browser
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] Allow the extensions platform on Android

Chrome for Android has never shipped extensions. `enable_extensions` is off for
`is_android` unconditionally in extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni, so no GN
argument can opt into it; the condition itself has to change.

The fork's own arguments go in build/config/wat.gni rather than into one of
Chromium's .gni files, so the change to Chromium proper is two lines: an import
and the condition. That is deliberate. Every line a fork touches is a conflict
waiting for the next release, and this patch has to survive a rebase every week.

GN forbids one argument in a declare_args() block from depending on another in
the same block, which is why `wat_android_extensions` cannot simply be declared
alongside `enable_extensions` — it arrives through the import instead.

Written against 151.0.7922.71. Never compiled: see chromium/README.md.
---
 build/config/wat.gni                | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni |  5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 build/config/wat.gni

diff --git a/build/config/wat.gni b/build/config/wat.gni
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/build/config/wat.gni
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Copyright 2026 The What-A-Browser Authors
+# Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style license that can be
+# found in the LICENSE file.
+
+# Build arguments belonging to the What-A-Browser fork.
+#
+# These live in a file of their own so the fork's arguments never have to be
+# threaded into Chromium's own .gni files. That keeps the rebase surface down to
+# the single line that imports this, which is the whole point: every line a fork
+# touches is a merge conflict waiting for the next release.
+
+declare_args() {
+  # Compiles the extensions platform into the Android build.
+  #
+  # Chrome for Android has never shipped extensions, and
+  # extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni switches `enable_extensions` off for
+  # `is_android` unconditionally. This opts back in.
+  #
+  # Upstream's own Android extensions work sits behind
+  # `enable_desktop_android_extensions`, which their comment calls
+  # "very much in-development, non-stable, and likely to crash at any given
+  # moment" (crbug.com/356905053). This rides the same code paths, so expect
+  # exactly that.
+  wat_android_extensions = false
+}
diff --git a/extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni b/extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni
--- a/extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni
+++ b/extensions/buildflags/buildflags.gni
@@ -3,10 +3,13 @@
 # found in the LICENSE file.
 
 import("//build/config/cast.gni")
 import("//build/config/chrome_build.gni")
+import("//build/config/wat.gni")
 
 declare_args() {
   # `enable_extensions` controls whether the full and stable extensions platform
   # is compiled.
-  enable_extensions = !is_android && !is_ios && !is_castos && !is_fuchsia
+  enable_extensions =
+      (!is_android || wat_android_extensions) && !is_ios && !is_castos &&
+      !is_fuchsia
 
